Iran's public prosecutor says the  country has shut down its so-called  morality police but local media has  pushed back against the comments saying  the interior Ministry oversees the force  and not the Judiciary Iran has been  rocked by months of protests sparked by  the death of a young woman in police  custody she was arrested allegedly for  not wearing her hijab properly the  demonstrations now in their third month  are the biggest challenge to the regime  in decades and protesters are calling  for a three-day strike this week  foreign  [Music]  Revolution changed the course of Iran  [Music]  authorities started monitoring the  adherence of the strict dress code  wearing the hijab hair covering became  mandatory in 1983.  over the years Iran saw several forms of  morality police  but the morality police known as kashte  irshad or guyton's petrol was  established under the hard-line former  president Mahmoud ahmediat  and the former president Hassan Rouhani  clothing Norms became slightly relaxed  but Iran's current president Ibrahim  raisi wanted a strict implementation of  the dress code  in September 22 year old Gina Masa amini  died three days after being taken into  custody by the morality police in Tehran  her alleged crime was not properly  covering her head  her death sparked unprecedented protests  across the country  Iranians demanded an end to the morality  police  they also called for a change of the  strict Islamic regime  I trembled when I heard the news because  this has happened to me once or twice it  made me think of how my parents would  feel if I suffered the same fate  I'm strongly against the morality police  because it is not possible to enforce a  cultural issue with violence  after nearly two months of protests in  which hundreds of people have been  killed the Iranian regime has signaled  some flexibility  Iran's Republic and Islamic foundations  are constitutionally entrenched  but there are methods of implementing  the Constitution that can be flexible  Iran now says it is disbanding the  morality police and is also debating the  implementation of the headscarf law  we're joined Now by Sarah bazubandi  she's a fellow at the German Institute  in global and area studies Giga Sarah  welcome to DW so what do you make of the  public Prosecutor's announcement that  Iran's morality police is being  disbanded  um I don't know if this is as your  report mentioned something to preempt  the  um what is about to happen if there is a  mass protester games take place  um in the next three days or it's just  the simple slip of the tongue and he  didn't really think about what he said  and it was right on the spot on a press  conference but in either case  um I I believe that the protesters find  a struggle to find anything positive or  meaningful in response to their demands  because they have a very clear from the  beginning of the  um protest that they are asking for a  regime change they're not asking for a  removal of head scarf and asking for  abolishment of the morality police they  want fundamental political change they  want separation of state and religion  and  hijab is only one element of that  separation so I and even if that was to  happen what he mentioned the law hasn't  changed so it really still um the law is  there  um implementation might change  temporarily but it doesn't really make  um much of a difference  Sarah would you say it's a sign though  of some sort that the authorities are  willing to heed the protesters demands  no I think that there is a certain level  of anxiety amongst the political Elites  uh because as again the report mentioned  this is one of the biggest challenges  since the revolution that Elite is  facing of course there is an effort to  find some response to call the public  down but at the same time time I don't  think that  um there is at this stage this can make  much of a difference as far as the  public is concerned so and maybe the  authorities in their own way think that  they are providing some form of  um  temporary measures to just calm the  public down but fundamentally it's it is  not really a response because this is  not what the people are asking for as  simple as they are now some are  skeptical about the announcement will  the morality police come back under  another name or a uniform are we just  going to basically not see much of a  change in that sense  foreign  as long as the law is there as I  mentioned it the implementation might  change might not it might come back in a  different form it might not and let's  not forget that even  um since the beginning of the protest  where the um presence the physical  presence of the morality police has been  a little bit  um less visible  um there are plain clothes regime  elements across public spaces that are  basically doing the same job as the  morality police was doing there are  millions of videos being circulated  um across Iranian users and social media  to show how a um plain clothes regime  elements in metros in bosses Industries  in restaurants are doing exactly the  same thing harassing women for the way  that they decided to choose their outfit  in public so implementation might change  we don't know yet but as long as the  lower the regulation is there it doesn't  really make much of a difference whether  that implementation is under a different  name whether it's done by plank levels  or officials  analyst Sarah basubandi from the German  Giga Institute thank you
 

